# Text files are LF in the repository and in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# Without this, a contributor's tooling decides: a Python script that writes a tracked
# file with the platform default newline turns `file.md` into CRLF on Windows and LF on
# Linux, and the diff - 600 to 2,500 lines for a content change of a dozen - passes every
# test, because nothing reads line endings. Two hooks now enforce what this declares.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Fuzz corpora and crash payloads are raw byte streams; the bytes ARE the
# input, so text normalization (CRLF handling) must never touch them. The
# committed seeds join the two ephemeral dirs: several carry a bare CR or a
# trailing space as part of the reproduction (`&"X-\r:`, `!y5%7c `), which a
# contributor with core.autocrlf enabled would otherwise silently rewrite.
fuzz/corpus/** -text
fuzz/artifacts/** -text
fuzz/seeds/** -text

# The two benchmark fixtures are data for the instruction-count gate, not prose. Their CR bytes sit
# *inside* raw strings, so a normalisation there changes the bytes the parser is handed.
#
# Measured, three views of the same path: `git show` of `main` stores this fixture with no CR bytes,
# `git show` of the branch that added `-text` stores it with 98, and a checkout on this Windows box
# carries 80 of its own. The stored bytes of the *measured input* therefore depended on which tree
# the last author committed - which is a reproducibility hole, not a formatting preference. `-text`
# closes the part that can be closed: conversion is refused in both directions, so the tree you
# measure from is the tree that was committed. `tests/test_line_endings_gate.py` asserts that
# equality, and `check_line_endings.py`'s exclusion is what it supports rather than replaces.
#
# Recorded so the next reader does not repeat the wrong inference: this was tested as the cause of
# the 1.45% gap between a WSL-measured Ir baseline and the runner's `serialize_block_scalars`, and
# it is NOT. Adding `-text` moved the runner's number by 28 instructions out of 16,020,906.
# `scripts/ir_gate.py` carries the measurements and the remaining open question.
crates/pyrs-yaml-core/src/bench_inputs.rs -text
crates/pyrs-yaml-core/benches/ir_gate.rs -text

# Binary: never diff or normalize.
*.pyd binary
*.so binary
*.pyd-* binary
*.dll binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
